1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,522
An engineer repaired a release branch in
her clone, her own copy of the repository,

2
00:00:09,562 --> 00:00:14,463
at eleven at night. A release branch is
the line of commits a release is cut from

3
00:00:14,503 --> 00:00:19,403
and fixed on. git status is clean, the
tests pass locally, and she wants to go to

4
00:00:19,443 --> 00:00:22,026
bed. Four questions
decide whether she may.

5
00:00:22,366 --> 00:00:27,203
Did the thing the user wanted happen? Did
the state that caused the problem change,

6
00:00:27,243 --> 00:00:31,846
for the reason she expected? Is the fix
on the server, and in the pull request,

7
00:00:31,886 --> 00:00:36,371
GitHub's proposal to merge the branch,
and in the pipeline run for that exact

8
00:00:36,411 --> 00:00:39,427
commit? And did anything
change that shouldn't have?

9
00:00:39,767 --> 00:00:44,684
A fix in your clone isn't a fix on the
server. The first check can pass while the

10
00:00:44,724 --> 00:00:49,151
third one fails. Hold on to that
sentence. Tonight it comes true for her.

11
00:00:50,991 --> 00:00:55,335
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:55,375 --> 00:00:59,144
This video closes Module 35
and the diagnosis method.

13
00:00:59,184 --> 00:01:04,391
You have the read-only phase from video
180 and video 181, the reading of

14
00:01:04,431 --> 00:01:09,926
interrupted operations from video 182,
and the preserve phase from video 183.

15
00:01:09,966 --> 00:01:15,460
What remains is the third phase: select
the lowest-risk fix, execute, verify,

16
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:21,426
prevent. From video 51 you have one table
and one decision tree for undoing things.

17
00:01:21,466 --> 00:01:25,451
Today's ladder is the same
idea, generalized to any fix.

18
00:01:25,791 --> 00:01:30,143
Then the symptom catalog: twenty-six
symptoms, each with its likely causes in the

19
00:01:30,183 --> 00:01:33,180
order worth testing and the
commands that separate them.

20
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:37,519
You don't memorize it. You learn how to
use it and what pattern runs through it.

21
00:01:37,859 --> 00:01:42,963
The terminal segment finishes the handover
of video 183: the half-done backport,

22
00:01:43,003 --> 00:01:48,300
with every layer of evidence already in
place. A backport copies a fix to a release

23
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:53,509
line, here with git cherry-pick, which
applies one commit's change to the current

24
00:01:53,549 --> 00:01:54,988
branch as a new commit.

25
00:01:56,828 --> 00:01:58,406
Here's what you'll walk away with.

26
00:01:58,746 --> 00:02:00,158
After this video you can:

27
00:02:00,498 --> 00:02:04,061
Rank candidate fixes by what each
can destroy and whom it affects.

28
00:02:04,401 --> 00:02:07,137
State the four checks that
make up a verification.

29
00:02:07,477 --> 00:02:11,106
Give an example where the first
check passes and a later one fails.

30
00:02:11,446 --> 00:02:15,702
Use the symptom catalog to go from an
error message to candidate causes.

31
00:02:16,042 --> 00:02:19,041
Turn a finished case into a
line of the team's checklist.

32
00:02:20,881 --> 00:02:25,649
Choosing a fix, in one sentence. List every
fix that would repair the root cause,

33
00:02:25,689 --> 00:02:30,397
rank them by what each can destroy and
how each is undone, and take the one that

34
00:02:30,437 --> 00:02:30,991
only adds.

35
00:02:31,331 --> 00:02:34,677
Precisely. Rank by five
questions, in this order.

36
00:02:35,017 --> 00:02:40,038
Does it destroy uncommitted work? That
work has no object and no reflog.

37
00:02:40,078 --> 00:02:45,662
Anything that can remove it, git reset
--hard, git restore, git clean, git stash

38
00:02:45,702 --> 00:02:50,933
drop, every --abort, ranks last unless
the working tree is clean or copied.

39
00:02:51,273 --> 00:02:54,269
Does it rewrite commits that
another repository has?

40
00:02:54,309 --> 00:02:59,056
Rewriting published history turns one
person's problem into every clone's problem.

41
00:02:59,396 --> 00:03:03,215
Does it change the server? A
local change is undone locally.

42
00:03:03,255 --> 00:03:08,424
A push, a deleted remote branch or a changed
rule is seen by others and may start

43
00:03:08,464 --> 00:03:09,067
workflows.

44
00:03:09,407 --> 00:03:14,417
Is it undone by one command? A new commit
is undone by a revert; a moved ref by

45
00:03:14,457 --> 00:03:17,166
moving it back; a pruned
object by nothing.

46
00:03:17,506 --> 00:03:22,973
Can it be previewed? --dry-run, git merge-tree,
git diff <target>, git log <range>,

47
00:03:23,013 --> 00:03:24,632
or a rehearsal in a copy.

48
00:03:24,972 --> 00:03:29,234
The result is a ladder of six
rungs, which is today's diagram.

49
00:03:29,274 --> 00:03:34,784
Prefer the lowest rung that repairs the
cause. In the case of video 180 that was

50
00:03:34,824 --> 00:03:40,196
rungs 1 and 2. In the case of video 181
it was rung 3, on commits that existed

51
00:03:40,236 --> 00:03:42,416
nowhere else, with rung 1 first.

52
00:03:42,756 --> 00:03:43,849
Three rules of thumb.

53
00:03:44,189 --> 00:03:49,190
On a shared branch, undo with git revert,
not with git reset, which moves the branch

54
00:03:49,230 --> 00:03:54,231
to another commit. A revert adds a commit.
Everyone's next pull is a fast-forward, a

55
00:03:54,271 --> 00:03:55,252
plain move ahead.

56
00:03:55,592 --> 00:04:01,272
When a ref must move back, use git reset
--keep. It refuses when a file with local

57
00:04:01,312 --> 00:04:05,249
changes would be overwritten,
where --hard overwrites it.

58
00:04:05,589 --> 00:04:10,855
When a force push, which overwrites the
server's branch, is the correct fix, state

59
00:04:10,895 --> 00:04:15,707
the expected old value: git push
--force-with-lease=<branch>:<expected-id>.

60
00:04:15,747 --> 00:04:20,172
The bare form is defeated by anything
that fetches in the background.

61
00:04:20,512 --> 00:04:25,643
Verification, in one sentence. A fix is
verified when the commands that showed the

62
00:04:25,683 --> 00:04:30,562
problem now show its absence, for the
reason you predicted, in every place the

63
00:04:30,602 --> 00:04:31,571
problem existed.

64
00:04:31,911 --> 00:04:33,516
Four checks, in this order.

65
00:04:33,856 --> 00:04:38,729
The symptom. Repeat the command or page
that showed it: the rejected push succeeds;

66
00:04:38,769 --> 00:04:41,333
the pull request lists
the expected commits.

67
00:04:41,673 --> 00:04:45,848
The state. Repeat the diagnosis
commands that exposed the cause.

68
00:04:45,888 --> 00:04:51,248
The first line of git status changed; the
brackets in git branch -vv changed; .git

69
00:04:51,288 --> 00:04:52,499
has no state files.

70
00:04:52,839 --> 00:04:58,256
Every copy. Compare IDs, not names: git
rev-parse HEAD @{upstream} and git ls-remote

71
00:04:58,296 --> 00:05:03,193
origin <branch> must print the same ID.
Then the colleague's clone, the pull

72
00:05:03,233 --> 00:05:06,311
request, the pipeline run
for that exact commit.

73
00:05:06,651 --> 00:05:11,658
Nothing else changed. git diff <backup> HEAD
or git range-diff shows that the result

74
00:05:11,698 --> 00:05:14,843
differs from the preserved
state only where intended.

75
00:05:14,883 --> 00:05:18,328
git fsck reports no damage.
git status shows no leftovers.

76
00:05:18,668 --> 00:05:23,444
Try it now. Thirty seconds, and it only
reads. In the lab shell, or in any

77
00:05:23,484 --> 00:05:27,088
repository you have, run git
rev-parse HEAD @{upstream}.

78
00:05:27,128 --> 00:05:30,603
The upstream is the server
branch your branch follows.

79
00:05:30,643 --> 00:05:33,401
Do you get two equal
IDs? Say what you see.

80
00:05:35,741 --> 00:05:40,134
Two equal IDs mean your branch agrees
with your record of its upstream.

81
00:05:40,174 --> 00:05:45,255
That record is the server at your last
fetch, so only git ls-remote tells you what

82
00:05:45,295 --> 00:05:50,375
the server holds now. And if Git answered
that no upstream is configured, that's a

83
00:05:50,415 --> 00:05:51,124
finding too.

84
00:05:51,464 --> 00:05:56,296
Write the prediction before you run the
check. Then, and only then, remove what the

85
00:05:56,336 --> 00:06:00,699
investigation added: the rescue branch,
the evidence directory, the bundle.

86
00:06:00,739 --> 00:06:04,162
git branch -D prints the ID,
which is one more safety line.

87
00:06:04,502 --> 00:06:09,243
What a pull request needs after a fix is
platform behavior: a push to the head

88
00:06:09,283 --> 00:06:13,963
branch reruns workflows and can dismiss
approvals. A forced push marks review

89
00:06:14,003 --> 00:06:18,866
comments as outdated. Verification includes
reading the pull request page again.

90
00:06:19,206 --> 00:06:22,877
The symptom catalog. Twenty-six
symptoms in five groups.

91
00:06:22,917 --> 00:06:27,581
History and refs. Working tree and
index. Remotes, access and identity.

92
00:06:27,621 --> 00:06:33,079
Pull requests, checks and CI. And submodules,
LFS and scale: a submodule is another

93
00:06:33,119 --> 00:06:38,645
repository checked out in a subdirectory of
yours, and Git LFS is a separate program

94
00:06:38,685 --> 00:06:44,210
that keeps large files outside your commits.
Each row gives the likely causes in the

95
00:06:44,250 --> 00:06:49,576
order worth testing, the commands that
distinguish them, and the chapter with the

96
00:06:49,616 --> 00:06:50,239
mechanism.

97
00:06:50,579 --> 00:06:55,263
How to use a row: the causes are your
ready-made hypotheses, already more than

98
00:06:55,303 --> 00:06:58,655
three, and each command is
the test that separates them.

99
00:06:58,695 --> 00:07:03,742
The catalog doesn't replace the opening
ritual. It replaces the blank page after it.

100
00:07:04,082 --> 00:07:08,950
The pattern across the catalog. The textbook
calls it the content of the book in one

101
00:07:08,990 --> 00:07:13,682
line: nearly every symptom is a difference
between two of the places of the state

102
00:07:13,722 --> 00:07:17,829
table that someone assumed were equal.
The working tree and the commit.

103
00:07:17,869 --> 00:07:21,042
The branch and HEAD. The local
branch and its upstream.

104
00:07:21,082 --> 00:07:25,249
The remote-tracking branch and the
server. The branch and the merge ref.

105
00:07:25,289 --> 00:07:27,293
The diagnosis is finding which two.

106
00:07:27,633 --> 00:07:32,221
When not to use the full method. A message
that names its own remedy, and whose

107
00:07:32,261 --> 00:07:36,849
remedy is green, needs no hypothesis
table: "The current branch has no upstream

108
00:07:36,889 --> 00:07:41,125
branch", with the command printed
beneath it, or a typo in a branch name.

109
00:07:41,165 --> 00:07:44,288
Run git status, do what the
message says, and move on.

110
00:07:44,328 --> 00:07:49,209
The full method is for symptoms that contradict
what the reporter believes about the

111
00:07:49,249 --> 00:07:53,427
state. The opening ritual is never
skipped: it takes less than a minute.

112
00:07:53,767 --> 00:07:58,651
When to stop and escalate. If the evidence
shows a credential in history, stop

113
00:07:58,691 --> 00:08:03,385
diagnosing and start the secret response:
revoke first, investigate second.

114
00:08:03,425 --> 00:08:08,625
If the repository reports corrupt or missing
objects, don't continue working in it:

115
00:08:08,665 --> 00:08:13,549
copy it and follow the procedure of
Chapter 13. If production is down, restore

116
00:08:13,589 --> 00:08:17,336
service first and diagnose
afterwards on preserved evidence.

117
00:08:17,676 --> 00:08:19,413
How the method itself fails.

118
00:08:28,053 --> 00:08:32,421
A picture helps. The ladder is a
physical picture, and it works as one.

119
00:08:32,461 --> 00:08:37,575
You climb only as high as the repair requires,
and each rung up is further to fall.

120
00:08:37,615 --> 00:08:42,231
On the first rung nothing can be lost.
On the sixth, nothing can be undone.

121
00:08:42,571 --> 00:08:46,609
Where the picture breaks: on a real
ladder the rungs are equally spaced.

122
00:08:46,649 --> 00:08:51,309
Here the gap between rung 3 and rung 4 is
much larger than the others, because it's

123
00:08:51,349 --> 00:08:54,933
the gap between "undone through
the reflog" and "unrecoverable".

124
00:08:54,973 --> 00:08:58,331
The reflog is Git's local record
of where each ref has been.

125
00:08:58,371 --> 00:09:02,805
And the gap between 4 and 5 is of a
different kind: from your own loss to other

126
00:09:02,845 --> 00:09:03,315
people's.

127
00:09:03,655 --> 00:09:06,947
For verification, think of a
doctor who ends a treatment.

128
00:09:06,987 --> 00:09:11,681
The patient says the pain is gone: the
symptom. The test that showed the cause is

129
00:09:11,721 --> 00:09:15,481
repeated: the state. Both lungs
are checked, not one: every copy.

130
00:09:15,521 --> 00:09:19,397
And nothing else was harmed by the
treatment: nothing else changed.

131
00:09:19,437 --> 00:09:24,015
A doctor who stops at the first check has
asked the patient, not examined them.

132
00:09:25,855 --> 00:09:30,723
Then place the interview question's five
fixes on it as a preview: each has a rung.

133
00:09:30,763 --> 00:09:33,029
I won't say which. That's the question.

134
00:09:37,369 --> 00:09:41,134
Choosing. Put the candidates on the
ladder before running anything.

135
00:09:41,174 --> 00:09:45,790
Continue the cherry-pick with the colleague's
resolution: rung 2, it adds commits.

136
00:09:45,830 --> 00:09:50,560
Abort and start again: an abort discards
the uncommitted resolution, which exists in

137
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:55,216
no object. The note says the operation was
intended and gives the intended result,

138
00:09:55,256 --> 00:09:59,589
so the resolution can be checked against
it. Continue is the lowest rung that

139
00:09:59,629 --> 00:10:00,611
repairs the cause.

140
00:10:00,951 --> 00:10:05,974
The fix. 🟡 CAUTION: git cherry-pick --continue
adds commits to the current branch.

141
00:10:06,014 --> 00:10:09,370
Preview: read the resolved
file against the note first.

142
00:10:09,710 --> 00:10:15,377
The file says 0.50 and 128, as the note
requires. Staging it marks the conflict

143
00:10:15,417 --> 00:10:21,157
resolved. The sequencer, Git's record of
the picks that remain, then commits the

144
00:10:21,197 --> 00:10:25,492
stopped pick and applies the
remaining one: two new commits.

145
00:10:25,832 --> 00:10:31,687
Verify. Predict before the output: in git
cherry -v release/1.4 main, which commits

146
00:10:31,727 --> 00:10:36,162
of main will carry a minus sign
and which a plus sign, and why?

147
00:10:36,202 --> 00:10:37,299
Say it out loud.

148
00:10:42,139 --> 00:10:46,412
Go through the checks. State: "On
branch", clean, and zero state files.

149
00:10:46,452 --> 00:10:50,846
The intended result: the committed
file has the two values from the note.

150
00:10:50,886 --> 00:10:55,766
Then git cherry: two commits of main are
marked with a minus sign, present on the

151
00:10:55,806 --> 00:10:59,836
release branch in equivalent form.
Two are marked with a plus sign.

152
00:10:59,876 --> 00:11:04,939
One of them was deliberately not backported.
The other, e0631de, was backported, and

153
00:11:04,979 --> 00:11:08,402
is marked as missing. Why? Say
your explanation out loud.

154
00:11:13,242 --> 00:11:17,136
This is the limit of the patch-ID
check that you met in video 172.

155
00:11:17,176 --> 00:11:22,145
It compares commits by their change, not by
their ID, and the port needed a conflict

156
00:11:22,185 --> 00:11:27,093
resolution, so its diff differs from the
original. A check that passes or fails has

157
00:11:27,133 --> 00:11:31,982
to be read with its limits. Here the evidence
that the port happened is the commit

158
00:11:32,022 --> 00:11:33,711
itself and the handover note.

159
00:11:34,051 --> 00:11:38,727
The failure scenario, in the rehearsal copy.
What would the other choice have done?

160
00:11:38,767 --> 00:11:43,217
🟡 CAUTION: git cherry-pick --abort resets
the index and the working tree to the

161
00:11:43,257 --> 00:11:44,126
starting commit.

162
00:11:44,466 --> 00:11:50,204
Quick quiz. After the abort, where is the
branch? A, on the first pick, the one that

163
00:11:50,244 --> 00:11:55,912
had succeeded. B, back before the whole
backport. And what does guard.yaml contain?

164
00:11:55,952 --> 00:11:56,738
Your answer?

165
00:12:01,578 --> 00:12:06,841
B. Clean, quiet, and wrong. The branch
is back at 329817a, before the whole

166
00:12:06,881 --> 00:12:12,073
backport, so even the pick that had
already succeeded has left the branch.

167
00:12:12,113 --> 00:12:16,174
And the file says 512: the
colleague's resolution is gone.

168
00:12:16,214 --> 00:12:19,498
It was never in an object,
so no reflog has it.

169
00:12:19,838 --> 00:12:21,726
The recovery, from what was preserved.

170
00:12:25,066 --> 00:12:29,092
The rescue branch brings back the
completed pick with a fast-forward.

171
00:12:29,132 --> 00:12:32,863
The cherry-pick is started again
and stops at the same conflict.

172
00:12:32,903 --> 00:12:37,636
The resolved file comes from the copy of
the repository, the one layer that holds

173
00:12:37,676 --> 00:12:39,580
uncommitted files. Then continue.

174
00:12:39,920 --> 00:12:41,494
Verification of the recovery.

175
00:12:41,834 --> 00:12:47,012
The commit IDs differ from the first repair.
The tree IDs, which name the complete

176
00:12:47,052 --> 00:12:51,212
snapshots, are equal: both repositories
end with the same content.

177
00:12:51,252 --> 00:12:55,094
That's the fourth check done
precisely: nothing else changed.

178
00:12:55,434 --> 00:12:59,769
Say the lesson of the scenario in one
sentence: the same mistake was made in the

179
00:12:59,809 --> 00:13:04,145
rehearsal that a tired engineer makes at
night, and it cost nothing, because two

180
00:13:04,185 --> 00:13:07,208
layers of preservation each
returned the part they hold.

181
00:13:09,048 --> 00:13:10,696
Five mistakes to watch for.

182
00:13:11,036 --> 00:13:13,797
Choosing the fix you know
instead of the lowest rung.

183
00:13:13,837 --> 00:13:18,077
Root cause: the candidates were never
listed and ranked by what each can destroy.

184
00:13:18,417 --> 00:13:24,346
Using git reset --hard where --keep would
do. Root cause: --hard overwrites files

185
00:13:24,386 --> 00:13:29,430
with local changes, and uncommitted
work has no object and no reflog.

186
00:13:29,770 --> 00:13:34,659
Stopping at "the symptom is gone". Root
cause: a fix in one clone is not a fix on

187
00:13:34,699 --> 00:13:39,465
the server, in the pull request or in the
pipeline, so the first check can pass

188
00:13:39,505 --> 00:13:40,926
while a later one fails.

189
00:13:41,266 --> 00:13:46,460
Comparing names instead of IDs. Root cause:
origin/<branch> is the server at the

190
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:51,236
last fetch; only an ID from git ls-remote
says what the server holds now.

191
00:13:51,576 --> 00:13:56,389
Reading a verification command without its
limits. Root cause: a patch-ID comparison

192
00:13:56,429 --> 00:14:01,011
reports a port that needed conflict
resolution as missing, so the mark alone can

193
00:14:01,051 --> 00:14:01,474
mislead.

194
00:14:03,314 --> 00:14:08,522
Now, out of the lab. The engineer from the
hook does the four checks before she goes

195
00:14:08,562 --> 00:14:12,146
to bed. The symptom: the
backported release branch builds.

196
00:14:12,186 --> 00:14:15,395
The state: no cherry-pick
in progress, clean status.

197
00:14:15,435 --> 00:14:20,518
Every copy: git rev-parse HEAD @{upstream}
prints two equal IDs, and git ls-remote

198
00:14:20,558 --> 00:14:24,517
origin prints a different one for
the branch. She hadn't pushed.

199
00:14:24,557 --> 00:14:28,328
Check three failed, at eleven
at night, with check one green.

200
00:14:28,368 --> 00:14:31,389
There's the sentence from
the opening, come true.

201
00:14:31,729 --> 00:14:36,225
She previews the push, pushes, compares
the three IDs again, and opens the pull

202
00:14:36,265 --> 00:14:40,761
request for the release: the push has
rerun the workflows, and one approval was

203
00:14:40,801 --> 00:14:45,584
dismissed, which she notes for the morning.
The fourth check: git range-diff against

204
00:14:45,624 --> 00:14:48,455
her rescue branch shows
only the intended commits.

205
00:14:48,795 --> 00:14:53,639
The next day the case becomes one line in
the team's checklist, written as a check

206
00:14:53,679 --> 00:14:58,643
and not as advice: "A fix is closed when
git ls-remote origin <branch> prints the ID

207
00:14:58,683 --> 00:15:02,515
you verified locally, and the
pipeline run for that ID is green."

208
00:15:04,355 --> 00:15:09,024
Your turn. Finish Lab 35.3,
"Preserve evidence, then fix", in

209
00:15:09,064 --> 00:15:15,431
lab-manual/m35-diagnosis-method.md: the
fix, the verification, the failure scenario

